{
  "id": "Compound::DB00126 - Gene::112398",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Vitamin C and Egln2, mediated through the interaction with proteins involved in L-ascorbic acid binding. Vitamin C binds to P3h2, which is involved in peptidyl-proline dioxygenase activity, and this pathway ultimately leads to Egln2. Similarly, Vitamin C binds to Ogfod2 and Plod2, both of which participate in L-ascorbic acid binding, which also leads to Egln2. These findings indicate that Vitamin C may influence Egln2 through a series of molecular interactions involving peptidyl-proline dioxygenase activity and L-ascorbic acid binding. The shared involvement of Egln2 in these pathways suggests a possible functional link, though the exact mechanism by which Vitamin C modulates Egln2 remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence supports a mechanistic pathway where Vitamin C exerts its effects through binding to proteins that ultimately interact with Egln2, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction and functional role of Vitamin C in Egln2 regulation.",
